# Fuller versions for Africa 1807 onward (override rw_af)
R = {
"Britain bans slave trade": ("PE",3,"Britain outlawed the slave trade and sent warships to hunt slavers off West Africa.","The Royal Navy's West Africa Squadron patrolled the coast from Senegal to Angola. Liberated captives were landed at Freetown in Sierra Leone, which grew into a community of people from all over West Africa.","Portuguese, Spanish and Brazilian slavers kept the trade going illegally for decades.","Freetown's Krio people and language descend from those liberated Africans."),
"Union of South Africa": ("P",2,"Britain merged its colonies and the defeated Boer republics into one white-ruled country.","Eight years after the Boer War, Britain created the Union of South Africa as a self-governing dominion. Only white men could vote in most of it, and the 1913 Natives Land Act soon restricted Black land ownership to about 7% of the country.","Black leaders founded the South African Native National Congress, later the ANC, in 1912 to fight back.","The land law's effects on who owns South Africa's land are still debated today."),
"WWI East Africa campaign": ("W",2,"World War I was fought across East Africa for four years, mostly by Africans.","German commander Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck led a small force of German officers and African askari soldiers on a long guerrilla chase through Tanzania, Kenya and Mozambique. He surrendered only after hearing of the armistice in Europe.","Over a million Africans were forced to carry supplies; hundreds of thousands of porters and civilians died of disease and hunger.","After the war, Tanganyika passed from Germany to Britain."),
"Egypt nominally independent": ("P",2,"Britain declared Egypt independent while keeping its troops, the canal and real power.","A nationwide revolution in 1919, led by Saad Zaghloul's Wafd party, forced Britain's hand. Egypt got a king and a parliament, but Britain kept control of defense, Sudan and the Suez Canal.","Egyptians resented the half-measure for 30 years until Nasser's 1952 revolution.","Egypt became the leading voice of Arab nationalism."),
"Italy invades Ethiopia": ("WP",3,"Mussolini conquered Ethiopia with poison gas, and the League of Nations did nothing.","Italy invaded to avenge its 1896 defeat at Adwa, using mustard gas on soldiers and civilians. Emperor Haile Selassie went to the League of Nations in person and warned, 'It is us today. It will be you tomorrow.'","Britain and Ethiopian fighters freed the country in 1941.","The League's failure showed Hitler that aggression would go unpunished."),
"Apartheid begins": ("P",3,"South Africa wrote total racial separation into law.","The National Party won the white-only election and passed laws classifying every person by race, banning mixed marriages, segregating neighborhoods and forcing Black South Africans to carry passes.","Resistance grew, from the 1960 Sharpeville massacre to the 1976 Soweto uprising, along with world sanctions.","Apartheid ended in 1994, but South Africa remains one of the most unequal countries on Earth."),
"Mau Mau uprising": ("WP",3,"Kenyan fighters rose up for land and freedom, and Britain answered with camps and torture.","White settlers had taken the best farmland in the Kenyan highlands. Kikuyu fighters of the Kenya Land and Freedom Army attacked settlers and loyalists. Britain declared an emergency, detained about 150,000 people and used widespread torture.","Military defeat for the fighters, but Britain saw it couldn't hold Kenya; independence came in 1963.","In 2013 Britain paid compensation to over 5,000 elderly survivors of the camps."),
"Algerian War": ("WP",3,"Algeria fought a brutal eight-year war to end 130 years of French rule.","The FLN began an armed uprising in 1954. France sent 400,000 troops, used torture and mass detention, and fought the FLN inside Algiers itself.","Algeria won independence in 1962; about a million European settlers fled to France.","The war still shapes French politics, immigration debates and French-Algerian relations."),
"Suez Crisis": ("PW",3,"Egypt took control of the Suez Canal and broke the last of Britain's imperial power.","Nasser nationalized the British- and French-owned canal. Britain, France and Israel secretly planned an invasion. The US, furious, forced them out by threatening Britain's currency.","Nasser became the hero of the Arab world; Britain's prime minister resigned.","It marked the moment the US replaced Britain and France as the dominant Western power in the Middle East."),
"Ghana independent": ("P",3,"Ghana became the first sub-Saharan African colony to win independence.","Kwame Nkrumah, jailed by the British, led a mass movement of strikes and boycotts. On March 6, 1957, the Gold Coast became Ghana, named after an ancient West African empire.","Nkrumah championed pan-Africanism and backed independence movements across the continent; he was overthrown in 1966.","Ghana's independence opened the floodgates of African decolonization."),
"'Year of Africa'": ("P",3,"Seventeen African countries became independent in a single year.","Nigeria, Congo, Senegal, Madagascar, Somalia and most of French West and Central Africa won freedom in 1960.","New states inherited colonial borders and economies built to export raw materials, and quickly became Cold War battlegrounds.","Most of Africa's map, and many of its political challenges, date from this moment."),
"Lumumba killed; Tanganyika independent": ("PW",3,"Congo's first elected leader was murdered, while Tanganyika won independence peacefully.","Patrice Lumumba, Congo's fiery prime minister, was overthrown months after independence and executed with Belgian help and CIA involvement. Across the continent, Julius Nyerere led Tanganyika to independence without bloodshed.","Congo slid into decades of dictatorship under Mobutu; Nyerere became one of Africa's most respected leaders.","Lumumba is a martyr of African independence; Belgium returned his only remain, a tooth, in 2022."),
"OAU founded; Kenya independent": ("P",3,"African nations created a continental union, and Kenya became independent.","Thirty-two states founded the Organization of African Unity in Addis Ababa to defend independence and end colonial rule. Kenya became independent under Jomo Kenyatta.","The OAU backed liberation movements in Angola, Mozambique, Rhodesia and South Africa.","It became today's African Union in 2002."),
"Tanzania formed": ("P",2,"Tanganyika and Zanzibar united to form Tanzania.","A violent revolution in Zanzibar overthrew the Arab-led sultanate in January 1964. Three months later Nyerere and Abeid Karume merged the two countries.","Tanzania became one of Africa's most stable states, with Swahili as a unifying national language.","Zanzibar keeps its own semi-autonomous government, and the union is still debated."),
"Biafran War; Arusha Declaration": ("WPI",3,"Nigeria went to war to stop Biafra breaking away, while Tanzania launched African socialism.","Igbo leaders declared Biafra independent after massacres in the north. Nigeria's blockade caused famine that horrified the world. The same year, Nyerere's Arusha Declaration promised Ujamaa: self-reliance and collective villages.","About 1–2 million died in Biafra; Ujamaa moved millions of Tanzanians into villages, with mixed results.","Biafra still shapes Nigerian politics; Ujamaa helped make Tanzanians see themselves as one nation."),
"Haile Selassie overthrown": ("P",2,"Ethiopia's emperor was deposed, ending one of the world's oldest monarchies.","Haile Selassie had ruled since 1930. Famine, a hidden cover-up and rising prices fed protests; the Derg, a Marxist military committee, took power.","Under Mengistu Haile Mariam, the 'Red Terror' killed tens of thousands.","Ethiopia's monarchy, which claimed descent from King Solomon, ended for good."),
"Angola & Mozambique independent": ("PW",2,"Portugal's African empire collapsed after its own revolution.","Portugal had fought costly wars against independence movements since 1961. When its dictatorship fell in 1974, it quickly left Angola and Mozambique.","Both fell into civil wars backed by the US, USSR, South Africa and Cuba; Angola's lasted until 2002.","Over a million people died in these wars."),
"Tanzania ousts Idi Amin": ("WP",3,"Tanzania invaded Uganda and toppled Idi Amin's brutal regime.","Amin's army invaded Tanzania's Kagera region in 1978. Nyerere's army, with Ugandan exiles, counterattacked and captured Kampala in April 1979.","Amin fled into exile; Uganda remained unstable for years.","It's one of the few times an African country invaded a neighbor to remove a dictator."),
"Zimbabwe independent": ("P",2,"White-ruled Rhodesia became Zimbabwe after a long bush war.","Rhodesia's white minority had declared independence from Britain in 1965 to avoid majority rule. A guerrilla war forced a deal, and Robert Mugabe won the 1980 elections.","Mugabe ruled for 37 years; land seizures and hyperinflation wrecked the economy.","Zimbabwe's story is often cited in debates about land reform."),
"Ethiopian famine": ("PE",2,"Famine killed up to a million Ethiopians, and Live Aid made the world pay attention.","Drought struck northern Ethiopia, but war and government policies made it far worse. BBC footage sparked Band Aid and the 1985 Live Aid concerts.","It changed how Western charities raise money.","Critics still debate how aid can be used as a weapon of war."),
"Mandela freed": ("P",3,"Nelson Mandela walked free after 27 years in prison.","Under sanctions, strikes and growing violence, President F.W. de Klerk legalized the ANC and released Mandela.","Four years of tense negotiations led to South Africa's first democratic election.","Mandela became a global symbol of forgiveness and peaceful transition."),
"Rwandan genocide; Mandela president": ("WP",3,"In 100 days, Rwanda's genocide killed about 800,000 people, while South Africa elected Mandela.","After the president's plane was shot down, Hutu extremists used radio and machetes to slaughter Tutsi and moderate Hutu. UN peacekeepers were ordered not to intervene. Weeks later, South Africa held its first free election.","Genocide perpetrators fled into Congo, starting the Congo wars.","The world's failure led to the 'Responsibility to Protect' principle; Rwanda rebuilt under Paul Kagame."),
"Congo wars begin": ("WE",3,"Congo's wars drew in nine countries and became the deadliest conflict since WWII.","Rwanda and Uganda invaded to chase genocide perpetrators and installed Laurent Kabila. Then they turned on him, and Angola, Zimbabwe and others joined. Armies plundered coltan, gold and diamonds.","Millions died, mostly from disease and hunger.","Fighting continues in eastern Congo, tied to minerals used in phones and electric cars."),
"African Union founded": ("P",2,"Africa replaced the OAU with a stronger union.","The AU was given power to intervene in member states in cases of genocide or war crimes, unlike the OAU.","It has sent peacekeepers to Somalia, Sudan and elsewhere.","The AU became a permanent G20 member in 2023."),
"Darfur": ("W",3,"Sudan's government and Janjaweed militias killed hundreds of thousands in Darfur.","After rebels attacked, the government armed Arab Janjaweed militias that burned non-Arab villages. About 300,000 died.","The International Criminal Court charged President Omar al-Bashir with genocide, the first sitting head of state charged.","Janjaweed leaders formed the RSF, now fighting Sudan's current war."),
"Arab Spring; South Sudan independent": ("PW",3,"Protests toppled North Africa's dictators, and South Sudan became the world's newest country.","A Tunisian street vendor's self-immolation sparked protests that toppled Ben Ali, then Mubarak in Egypt, then Gaddafi in Libya with NATO help. South Sudan voted overwhelmingly to secede from Sudan.","Libya fell into civil war; South Sudan into its own civil war by 2013.","Tunisia's democracy backslid after 2021; South Sudan remains fragile."),
"Ebola outbreak": ("S",2,"Ebola killed about 11,000 people in West Africa.","The virus spread through Guinea, Liberia and Sierra Leone, where health systems had been weakened by war.","The world built faster ways to respond to epidemics.","An Ebola vaccine was approved in 2019."),
"Tigray war": ("W",2,"Ethiopia's war in Tigray killed hundreds of thousands.","Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed, a Nobel Peace Prize winner, sent troops against the TPLF, Tigray's ruling party. Eritrean forces joined. Famine was used as a weapon.","A peace deal was signed in 2022.","It stalled Ethiopia's economic rise."),
"Sahel coups": ("PW",2,"Military coups swept the Sahel, pushing out France and bringing in Russia.","Juntas took power in Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger, blaming civilians and French troops for failing to stop jihadist insurgents.","France withdrew its troops; Russian mercenaries moved in; the juntas left the regional bloc ECOWAS.","Jihadist violence in the Sahel has kept growing."),
"Sudan civil war": ("W",3,"Sudan's two generals went to war, creating the world's largest displacement crisis.","The army and the RSF shared power after ousting Bashir, then fought over control. Darfur saw new massacres.","Over 10 million people have been displaced and famine has spread.","It's one of the world's worst humanitarian crises today."),
}
